==== Installation

Use source code in this tarball. Compile, install, and operate Yate and YateBTS
based on these instructions:

https://github.com/Nuand/bladeRF/wiki/Setting-up-Yate-and-YateBTS-with-the-bladeRF#Building_and_Installing_Yate__YateBTS

==== Running

Before starting Yate, load the FPGA. Do not rely on autoload feature.
Find the appropriate RBF for your device's version and size, for example the
bladeRF 2.0 micro xA4 can be loaded as follows:

bladeRF-cli -l hostedxA4.rbf
sleep 1
sudo yate

If yate hangs or freezes, try to exit the program and run
`sudo killall -9 mbts yate`. Reload the FPGA, and rerun yate.
If problems persist plug the device into another USB port.

==== Fine tuning

Yate's architecture is to generate as many future TX bursts as possible
until the TX fifos start blocking. Essentially RadioInterface::send() is
supposed to block until there is enough buffer room between
libusb->FX3->FPGA to fit another 1024 sample buffer.

Yate's expects to generate TX burst roughly 2 subframes before their
RF transmission. To calculate how many samples 2 subframes correspond to,
multiply the following:

   2 subframes
   8 timeslots / subframe
   156.25 symbols / timeslot
   8 IQ samples / symbol

   2*8*156.25*8= 20,000 IQ samples

24 buffers * 1024 samples is about 24576 samples, which (factoring in
some processing lag) corresponds to Yate's expectation of sending TX
bursts 2 sub frames before RF transmission.

The buffer size, and num buffers, num_transfers numbers were chosen
based on this methodlogy, however they might need to be tuned depending
on the USB host controller, CPU, and OS settings.

HOW TO TUNE:
Define '#define BRF_DEBUG' at the top of ybladerf.cpp and recompile and
install yate.


Strong deviations from 20,000 samples is not good, however issues really
only arise (and they do so visibly) if the time difference number drops
below 5,000 (or goes negative) TX bursts will not make it to the FPGA in
time to be sent over the SMA port. In this case, consider increasing the
"num buffers" and "num transfers" in unison.

If CPU throughput is the issue, multiply buffer_size by a factor of 2,
and divide "num buffers" and "num transfers" by the same number.

Increasing "num transfers" is helpful if USB timeout errors appearing.
Care must be taken to not increase this number past 50% of "num buffers"


==== L3 message injection (mbts testinject) with pycrate

The mbts "testinject" command opens a UDP socket so you can inject raw Layer 3
frames (MM, CC, RR, SMS) onto an active dedicated channel and read the handset
replies. Use it to interact with or fuzz a phone in a controlled test setup
(your own handset, shielded environment).

Reaching the command: mbts commands are issued from the Yate rmanager console
with the "mbts" prefix. A bare command goes to Yate (and may hit the Eliza demo
script), not to mbts. So use "mbts testinject", not "testinject".

Difference from OpenBTS: mbts does not run call control (Yate does). A dedicated
channel exists only while the MS holds one for a transaction (location update,
SMS, or a call). testinject can create that channel for you by paging.

Usage:
  mbts testinject                           list active dedicated channels + index
  mbts testinject <index> [port] [sapi]     inject onto active channel <index>
  mbts testinject wait [port] [sapi]        block for the next channel, then inject
  mbts testinject page <IMSI> [port] [sapi] page the IMSI onto an SDCCH, then inject

Defaults: port 28670, sapi 0. SAPI 0 carries MM/CC/RR, SAPI 3 carries SMS CP/RP.
Send the text "STOP" over the socket to end a session.

Getting a channel, three ways:

1. page mode (closest to the OpenBTS testcall behaviour):
     mbts testinject page 001010000000001
   mbts pages the IMSI; the MS answers with a Paging Response that establishes
   an SDCCH, and testinject latches onto it. The channel can be short-lived
   because Yate has no matching transaction and will release it, but the latch
   still gives an injection window. List attached handsets with:
     nipc list registered

2. wait mode plus an externally triggered transaction:
     mbts testinject wait
   then trigger something: dial a number on the handset, or place a call or send
   an SMS to it from the NIB web interface. testinject grabs the new channel.

3. index mode, when a transaction is already up:
     mbts testinject       (shows the index)
     mbts testinject 0     (inject onto channel 0)

Python example (needs pycrate: pip install pycrate):

    #!/usr/bin/env python3
    import socket
    from pycrate_mobile.TS24008_MM import MMIdentityRequest
    from pycrate_mobile.TS24008_IE import IDTYPE_IMSI
    from pycrate_mobile.NAS import parse_NAS_MO

    BTS_IP = "127.0.0.1"
    PORT   = 28670            # mbts testinject default

    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_DGRAM)
    sock.settimeout(3)

    def inject(raw):
        "Send raw L3 bytes to the handset, return the raw reply (or b'')."
        sock.sendto(raw, (BTS_IP, PORT))
        try:
            data, _ = sock.recvfrom(1500)
            return data
        except socket.timeout:
            return b""

    # Craft a downlink MM Identity Request asking for the IMSI.
    req = MMIdentityRequest(val={'IDType': IDTYPE_IMSI})
    print("inject:", req.to_bytes().hex())     # e.g. 051801
    resp = inject(req.to_bytes())

    if resp and resp != b"TIMEOUT":
        print("reply :", resp.hex())
        msg, err = parse_NAS_MO(resp)          # decode the uplink reply
        if not err:
            print(msg.show())
    else:
        print("no reply (TIMEOUT or channel gone)")

    sock.sendto(b"STOP", (BTS_IP, PORT))       # end the injection loop

Craft any other message the same way, for example CC messages from
pycrate_mobile.TS24008_CC (Setup, Alerting, Disconnect, ...) or RR messages.
Whatever bytes you send are transmitted to the handset without modification.
For SMS (CP/RP) use SAPI 3, for example: mbts testinject page <IMSI> 28670 3
